<div class="start-card"><p><strong>You will learn:</strong> how to make Azure identity, regional availability and quota explicit before starting expensive work.</p><p><strong>Starting requirements:</strong> a suitable Azure subscription and permission to create resources and role assignments. Software installation is preparation time, not part of the four-hour estimate.</p><p><strong>Shell and directory:</strong> Bash, at the repository root. On Windows use Ubuntu 24.04 in WSL2, with Docker Desktop's WSL integration enabled for that distribution. Keep this environment for every Azure command.</p></div>
<h2 id="before-you-begin">Have these ready before the workshop</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Azure account and subscription:</strong> a Microsoft or work/school account that can sign in to an active Azure subscription. An account alone is not a subscription. Confirm billing or credits cover the selected services, accept the <a href="./reference.html#costs">estimated costs</a>, and choose a development subscription you can clean up afterward. A free/trial subscription does not guarantee the required model or VM quota.</li><li><strong>Azure permissions:</strong> Owner, or Contributor plus Role Based Access Control Administrator, at subscription scope. Arrange this with the subscription owner before starting. You need to create resources, register providers and assign identities; tenant administration alone does not grant these permissions.</li><li><strong>Capacity and model access:</strong> the chosen region must support AKS, PostgreSQL, Functions Flex and the selected chat/embedding models. The checker below verifies offers and quotas; the next chapter makes real model calls before starting AKS. Resolve organizational restrictions and quota requests in advance.</li><li><strong>A development computer:</strong> a browser, the supplied repository files and a terminal. For the supplied Windows installer use supported x64 Windows 11 with hardware virtualization enabled and local administrator access. We recommend 16 GB RAM and 30 GB free disk space for WSL, images and build caches. Follow <a href="https://docs.docker.com/desktop/setup/install/windows-install/">Docker's current system requirements</a> if using another supported Windows configuration.</li><li><strong>Network access:</strong> allow software/package downloads, Azure sign-in and Azure service endpoints. Know whether a corporate proxy, VPN or device policy blocks Docker/WSL, package installation or cloud resource creation.</li><li><strong>Local software:</strong> the tools listed below, installed in the same Bash environment, and a running Linux-container Docker engine. No pre-existing AKS cluster, database or other Azure resources are required.</li></ul>
<h2 id="windows-setup">Windows: download the setup script</h2>
<p><strong>Yes, the documented Windows path uses Docker Desktop.</strong> It provides the local Linux-container engine for building, running, tagging and pushing images. Start it during the workshop. On native Linux an existing Docker Engine 24+ with BuildKit is sufficient. Merely reading or serving this documentation does not require Docker or Azure access.</p>
<p><a class="download-link" href="./scripts/setup-windows.ps1" download="setup-windows.ps1">Download Windows setup (.ps1)</a> · <a href="./scripts/setup-ubuntu.sh" download="setup-ubuntu.sh">Download Ubuntu tool setup (.sh)</a>. Review {{file:scripts/setup-windows.ps1}} and {{file:scripts/setup-ubuntu.sh}} before running them. They install local software only and do not sign in, register Azure providers or create cloud resources. Review <a href="https://docs.docker.com/desktop/setup/install/windows-install/">Docker Desktop's license terms</a> for your organization. The WinGet invocation accepts package/source agreements; application first-run setup remains interactive.</p>
<ol><li>Open <strong>PowerShell as Administrator</strong>, change to the folder containing the downloaded <code>setup-windows.ps1</code> (normally Downloads), and run the command below. Add <code>-WhatIf</code> to preview the actions. The process-only execution-policy flag permits this reviewed script without changing the machine's saved execution policy. If WinGet is missing, install/update Microsoft's <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/package-manager/winget/">App Installer</a> first.</li></ol>
{{snippet:scripts/setup-windows.ps1:run}}
<ol start="2"><li>The script uses <strong>WinGet</strong> to install Docker Desktop and <code>wsl --install --distribution Ubuntu-24.04 --no-launch</code> to enable/install WSL and Ubuntu. Existing installations are preserved. Restart if Windows requests it, then rerun the script to finish. Allow download/restart time before the four-hour core path.</li><li>Open Ubuntu 24.04 from Start, or run <code>wsl -d Ubuntu-24.04</code>. Create a Linux username/password on first launch. In PowerShell, <code>wsl --list --verbose</code> must show version <strong>2</strong> for this distribution.</li><li>Start Docker Desktop. Select the <strong>WSL2 engine</strong> and <strong>Linux containers</strong>; under <strong>Settings → Resources → WSL Integration</strong>, enable <strong>Ubuntu-24.04</strong> and apply the change. A local Kubernetes cluster in Docker Desktop is unnecessary.</li><li>In <strong>Ubuntu Bash</strong>, copy/extract the supplied repository into a Linux folder such as <code>~/ai200</code>, then open that repository root. You should see <code>index.html</code> and <code>workshop/</code>. A Windows checkout can also be reached through <code>/mnt/c/</code>, although a Linux filesystem copy avoids slower bind mounts and line-ending issues. Run the supplied Ubuntu setup from the repository, so it can also install the workshop's Python requirements:</li></ol>
{{snippet:commands.sh:setup-ubuntu}}
<p>The Ubuntu script uses apt for Java 21, Python 3.12, Bash, Git, jq, curl, OpenSSL, archive tools and Azure CLI from Microsoft's signed repository. It installs Bicep 0.49.2 and checksum-verifies kubectl 1.34.1. It creates <code>workshop/.tools/venv</code> for the pinned client dependencies. It does not install a second Docker daemon inside WSL. Windows Azure CLI, Python and Java installations do not substitute for these Linux tools.</p>
<p>Have the tools already? Skip installation and run this local check, which requires neither Azure sign-in nor bootstrap state:</p>
{{snippet:commands.sh:local-check}}
<h2 id="tools">Prepare one environment</h2>
<p>Install <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/install">WSL2</a> and enable <a href="https://docs.docker.com/desktop/features/wsl/">Docker Desktop integration</a> if you use Windows. Run the commands inside Ubuntu, not a PowerShell tab. macOS and Linux Bash also work if the listed tools are available. Images target <code>linux/amd64</code>, matching the supplied AKS node.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Required role/version</th><th>Check in Bash</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Python + venv/pip</td><td>3.12+ for local tooling; deployed Python is 3.12</td><td><code>python3 --version</code></td></tr><tr><td>Java</td><td>21 LTS; Maven 3.9.11 is supplied by Wrapper and Docker</td><td><code>java -version</code></td></tr><tr><td>Azure CLI + Bicep</td><td>CLI 2.91+; installer pins Bicep 0.49.2</td><td><code>az version</code>, <code>az bicep version</code></td></tr><tr><td>Docker</td><td>A running Linux-container engine 24+ with BuildKit; Docker Desktop + WSL2 integration on Windows</td><td><code>docker info</code></td></tr><tr><td>kubectl</td><td>1.32+; installer uses 1.34.1. Stay within one minor of the deployed AKS version; adjust the client after checking <code>kubectl version</code> if needed.</td><td><code>kubectl version --client</code></td></tr><tr><td>Other commands</td><td>Bash 4+, Git, jq 1.6+, curl, OpenSSL 3, zip/unzip</td><td>The checker tests their presence</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Use Microsoft's <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cli/azure/install-azure-cli-linux?pivots=apt">Azure CLI installation instructions</a> and <code>az bicep install</code>. Use <code>az aks install-cli</code> if you need kubectl. Helm, a local Functions host and a local PostgreSQL installation are not required for the core path: Azure CLI handles Function package deployment; AKS supplies KEDA.</p>
<p>Sign in with <code>az login</code>, select the intended subscription using <code>az account set --subscription ...</code>, then inspect <code>az account show</code>. This is the one subscription choice to make deliberately. Do not continue if the selected subscription belongs to a customer or production environment.</p>
<h2 id="bootstrap">Create the small bootstrap configuration</h2>
{{snippet:commands.sh:bootstrap}}
<p>The bootstrap tool reads the selected subscription, chooses <code>swedencentral</code>, and creates names from the prefix <code>raglab</code> plus a subscription-derived suffix. It detects the calling network's public IPv4 and restricts the Products service to that <code>/32</code>. Review the printed non-secret configuration.</p>
<p>Use <code>--subscription</code>, <code>--location</code>, <code>--prefix</code> or <code>--allowed-cidr</code> on {{file:tools/bootstrap.py}} when needed, or provide a reviewed copy of {{file:config.example.json}} with <code>--config</code>. The prefix must contain 3–8 lowercase letters/digits and start with a letter; generated Storage, ACR and Key Vault names respect their length/character constraints. Globally unique names may still be reserved or soft-deleted: change the prefix before creating resources if Azure reports a collision.</p>
<p><code>workshop/.state/config.json</code> contains non-secret resume state and is ignored by Git. Scripts reuse it rather than silently switching subscriptions. Do not edit its subscription or prefix after creating resources. The website never reads it and never asks you to put credentials in browser storage or URLs.</p>
<p>If several participants share one Azure subscription, give each person a distinct prefix before provisioning. The default prefix intentionally reuses the same workshop resources in that subscription; it is not per-browser isolation.</p>
<h2 id="permissions">Read and fix the checker results</h2>
<p>{{file:tools/preflight.py}} checks tool versions, Docker, sign-in, subscription state, provider registration, creation/role-assignment permissions, regional service offers, a compatible VM SKU, CPU quota including upgrade surge, and model offers/quota. It stops on unknown critical quota results; a missing API response is not evidence of capacity.</p>
<p>Use <strong>Owner</strong>, or <strong>Contributor plus Role Based Access Control Administrator</strong>, at subscription scope for this workshop. A resource-scoped Contributor alone cannot register providers or assign the required identities. Organizational policy and deny assignments can still block operations even when these roles are present.</p>
<p>If providers are unregistered, the following explicitly registers them, waits with a bounded readiness check, and reruns the checks. Unregistered Compute or model providers can also make quota/offer queries return empty results, so register first rather than assuming that empty results prove no quota:</p>
{{snippet:commands.sh:providers}}
<div class="warning notice"><p><strong>Expensive blockers:</strong> one D4ds_v5 node needs four vCPUs, and the check reserves another four for an upgrade surge. Confirm PostgreSQL Burstable capacity and Flex regional quota in the portal if your subscription restricts them. Model listings are not proof of inference access; chapter 3 creates only the model resource first and makes actual calls before AKS starts.</p></div>
<h2 id="local-tools">Prepare the small verification environment</h2>
{{snippet:commands.sh:tools}}
<p>This virtual environment is for smoke/load tools and local tests. It does not change the Python installed by Azure Functions. To resume in another terminal, run <code>source workshop/.tools/venv/bin/activate</code>. Container builds install their own pinned requirements.</p>
<h2 id="verify">Expected results and common failures</h2>
<div class="verification"><strong>Verification:</strong> the checker should end with “All read-only gates passed.” This is expected output, not a claim about your subscription. At this point no paid resources should have been created.</div>
<ul><li><strong>Docker daemon unavailable:</strong> start Docker Desktop, enable this WSL distribution, reopen Ubuntu and retry <code>docker info</code>.</li><li><strong>Java version is not 21:</strong> install OpenJDK 21 and set <code>JAVA_HOME</code> and <code>PATH</code> in this Bash session. A Windows JDK outside WSL does not install a Linux JDK.</li><li><strong>Quota/API response missing:</strong> use Azure portal → Subscription → Usage + quotas. Ask the owner to increase the chosen VM family and model quota, or choose a region offering all required services. Do not bypass the check by reducing it to zero.</li><li><strong>Role assignment write denied:</strong> ask the subscription owner for the role stated above. Tenant admin consent is different from Azure resource RBAC.</li><li><strong>Your public IP changes:</strong> stop and update the allowed <code>/32</code> before applying the service. A VPN can change which address Azure sees.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Continue when:</strong> all read-only checks pass, the subscription is correct and the cost explanation in the next chapter is acceptable.</p>
